whileT -- SEQ 475 (Tony, 2026-10-30): WHILe on the new road, PRE-REGISTERED ON TRUNK before the build.

This file is not run alone. pop.sh builds two inputs per row, one process each under an alarm: everything above this file's
`stop();`, then the row on the OLD road (the statement itself) or on the NEW road (the rule called by name, then testOP on
what it handed back, except WH-0, which builds and does not run), then `WH NEXT` and a sentinel. Read by value, the old
road's print as the control. wc is declared, so every row starts from 0 in its own process. EXPRESSIOn's `Assign at end`
debug line is filtered from the value.

TODAY (2026-10-30, measured): WHILe has no action, so testOP refuses its label (WH-1, WH-2). A multi-statement body is a
BLOCk, which has no action and is defer; break and continue are BRANCh, which has no action and is defer -- so neither hands
up an instruct, and If's action (SEQ 473) attaches a null for an arm that is a break or a block: WH-3 and WH-4 die with exit
139 in the parse. WH-5's STARt seq skips the WHILe and prints 7 8.

RIDER (SEQ 475): WH-6 nested loops, the inner breaks at 2 and the outer runs every pass; WH-7 no leak, a print after a
breaking loop in the same seq runs; WH-8 return through a loop -- on the new road an action body is not built yet (rung 5),
so the action is a STARt, its testOP the call, and the 4th field is an OLD-road statement run after the call: the print after
the call must run (branchKind must not leak into the old road's block). The old-road control is the real action wr.

